# WSJTX AutoPilot

Windows companion for observing WSJT-X UDP traffic and evaluating a minimal
FT8/FT4 AutoCall policy. Armed control can initiate a QSO from a decoded CQ/QRZ
using the documented WSJT-X Reply packet. Milestone 3 optionally supports calls
addressed directly to the local station when used with the documented patched
WSJT-X Improved build.

> **Safety warning:** the default mode is observation/dry-run only. Armed
> control sends a WSJT-X Reply equivalent to double-clicking an exact Decode.
> Depending on WSJT-X state, this may enable or initiate transmission. The GUI
> always starts DISARMED. No CAT, Hamlib, PTT, or complete automatic QSO
> sequence is implemented.

## Requirements

- Windows 10/11
- Python 3.12 or newer
- WSJT-X using network protocol schema 2 or 3

## Install

In PowerShell from this directory:

```powershell
py -3.12 -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -e ".[test]"
```

For the desktop interface and executable build tools:

```powershell
python -m pip install -e ".[test,gui,build]"
```

## Configure WSJT-X

1. Open **File > Settings > Reporting** in WSJT-X.
2. Under **UDP Server**, set **Server name or IP address** to `127.0.0.1`.
3. Set **Server port number** to `2237`.
4. Enable **Accept UDP requests**. This is only needed for explicitly armed
   Reply control; dry-run sends nothing.
5. Apply the settings. Do not configure another program on the same local UDP
   port unless it supports forwarding or multicast.

If WSJT-X runs on another computer, start this application with `--bind 0.0.0.0`
and point WSJT-X at the Windows computer's LAN address. Adjust Windows Firewall
to permit inbound UDP port 2237 on the appropriate private network only.

## Run

Desktop interface:

```powershell
wsjtx-autopilot --gui
```

Preferences are stored in `%APPDATA%\WSJTX-AutoPilot\settings.json`. The GUI
shows live Decode activity, explicit candidate score components, QSO state and
WSJT-X connection state. ARM CONTROL always requires an operator confirmation;
arm state is never saved. DISARM blocks new initiations without stopping QSO
observation.

Command-line dry-run:

```powershell
wsjtx-autopilot
```

This command is always dry-run. It may print `[WOULD_ACTION]`, but sends no UDP
Reply.

For the first controlled live test, use the one-shot limit:

```powershell
wsjtx-autopilot --control --arm-auto-reply --max-actions 1
```

Both `--control` and `--arm-auto-reply` are required. With only one flag, the
application remains dry-run. Armed startup prints:

```text
[AUTO CONTROL ARMED]
WSJT-X Reply packets may initiate transmissions
```

Create a file named `DISARM_AUTOPILOT` in the working directory to trigger the
software kill switch while leaving the listener running. A different path can
be selected with `--kill-switch-file`. The one-shot command disarms new
initiations after its first successfully sent Reply. A failed local `sendto()`
does not consume an action.

Options:

```text
--callsign F4NJU       Local callsign (default: F4NJU)
--bind 127.0.0.1       UDP bind address
--port 2237            UDP port
--stale-seconds 15     Maximum decode age
--worked-store data/autopilot.sqlite3  Persistent Worked Today database
--wsjtx-log PATH       Explicit wsjtx_log.adi path for startup synchronization
--allow-dupes          Permit same-day/same-band duplicate initiations
--dry-run-cooldown-seconds 90  Delay before proposing the same station again
--control               Enable the control adapter (does not arm it alone)
--arm-auto-reply        Arm Reply packets (requires --control)
--wsjtx-direct-reply-patched  Permit direct Reply with the patched WSJT-X only
--direct-reply-confirmation-timeout 20  Wait for coherent Status evidence
--max-actions 1         Maximum successfully sent Reply initiations
--kill-switch-file DISARM_AUTOPILOT  Runtime software kill switch
--record capture.bin   Record timestamped UDP datagrams
--replay capture.bin   Replay a capture without opening the UDP port
--verbose              Debug logging
```

The listener displays Heartbeat, Status, and Decode packets. Valid recent CQ
messages are collected briefly; an unambiguous call addressed to the local
callsign takes priority while the state machine is idle. A selected candidate
produces output such as:

```text
[ENGINE] direct caller detected: ON4ABC
[WOULD_ACTION] direct-reply ON4ABC
```

## Tests

```powershell
python -m pytest
```

Build the windowed executable after installing the build extras:

```powershell
pyinstaller packaging/wsjtx-autopilot.spec
```

The executable is written to `dist\WSJTX-AutoPilot.exe`.

## Reply Control

Reply type 4 reproduces the selected Decode's original instance id, schema,
time, SNR, delta time, delta frequency, raw mode marker, message, and
low-confidence flag. It is sent only to the source endpoint associated with the
same observed WSJT-X instance. Clear invalidates pending Decodes.

Stock WSJT-X accepts Reply only when it exactly matches a CQ/QRZ Decode still
present in its decode window. Direct Reply is therefore disabled by default.
It requires the minimal patch and build procedure in
[`docs/WSJTX_DIRECT_REPLY.md`](docs/WSJTX_DIRECT_REPLY.md), plus the explicit
`--wsjtx-direct-reply-patched` option.

First direct-call test, always one-shot:

```powershell
wsjtx-autopilot --control --arm-auto-reply --wsjtx-direct-reply-patched --max-actions 1
```

After a Direct Reply UDP send, AutoPilot waits in `DIRECT_REPLY_SENT`. It only
enters `CALLING_STATION` after a coherent Status combines the expected DX Call
with transmitting state or matching enabled TX message. DX Call alone is not
confirmation. A timeout produces `DIRECT_REPLY_UNCONFIRMED` and safely resets
to `IDLE`, which also detects a stock WSJT-X that ignored the packet.

Once a CQ or Direct Reply is sent, WSJT-X Auto Seq remains solely responsible
for reports, R-reports, RR73, 73, and logging. AutoPilot observes those exchanges
without sending additional Reply packets. A matching QSOLogged type 5 is the
strong completion event: the session transitions through `COMPLETE`, clears its
remote station and old candidates, and returns to `IDLE`. RR73/73 can also mark
completion; AutoPilot waits for inactive TX status and a short grace period
before returning to `IDLE` when QSOLogged is absent.

`--max-actions` counts only CQ Reply and Direct Reply datagrams successfully
sent by AutoPilot. Auto Seq transmissions do not consume actions. Reaching the
limit blocks new initiations but does not interfere with completion tracking for
the QSO already started.

## Worked Today

Automatic initiations are filtered by default using this key:

```text
UTC date + normalized callsign + canonical amateur band
```

For example, working `YO6LM` on 20m blocks another automatic CQ or Direct Reply
to `YO6LM` on any 20m frequency until 00:00 UTC. The same station remains
eligible on 40m. FT8 versus FT4 does not change duplicate status.

Records are persisted in `data/autopilot.sqlite3` by default. Every valid
QSOLogged packet is recorded, including QSOs initiated manually in WSJT-X.
Reply transmission alone never marks a station as worked; failed or timed-out
QSOs therefore do not create duplicate records.

At startup AutoPilot imports `wsjtx_log.adi` when one unambiguous Windows
WSJT-X profile is found under `%LOCALAPPDATA%`. Use `--wsjtx-log PATH` when the
log is elsewhere or multiple profiles exist. The ADIF import reads only CALL,
QSO_DATE, BAND/FREQ, and MODE/SUBMODE data and is idempotent. A missing log emits
a warning but does not prevent startup.

Use `--allow-dupes` to bypass only this Worked Today filter. Active-QSO,
cooldown, stale Decode, low-confidence, max-actions, and datagram duplicate
protections remain enabled.

## Current Limits

- Documented WSJT-X schema 2/3 packet types 0 through 5 needed by this milestone
  are supported.
- Only common structured FT8/FT4 CQ, grid, report, RRR, RR73, and 73 messages
  are accepted; ambiguous free text is deliberately ignored.
- Direct-call response requires the separately built, visibly identified
  `AutoPilot-DR1` WSJT-X Improved binary. Complete QSO sequencing, logging,
  CAT/radio control and full QSO sequencing do not exist.
- QSO decisions are in-memory and are lost when the process exits.
